<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Package versions are <dd-sdk-ios version>.<binding revision>, e.g. 3.14.0.1 - the same
      scheme the two platform binding repositories use, and deliberately the *iOS* line.

      This repository binds nothing itself. It is a cross-platform façade over three independently
      versioned package sets:

        DatadogNet.iOS      3.14.0.x   -> dd-sdk-ios     3.14.0
        DatadogNet.Android  3.12.1.x   -> dd-sdk-android 3.12.1
        DatadogNet.Mac      3.14.0.x   -> dd-sdk-ios     3.14.0, built for Mac Catalyst

      These never move in lock-step: Datadog releases the iOS and Android SDKs on separate
      cadences and the version numbers have never matched. A façade therefore has to pick one line
      to name itself after or invent a third numbering nobody can map back to anything. It picks
      iOS, because that is the line whose version is furthest ahead and so the one a reader is most
      likely to recognise, and states the Android version everywhere it states its own.

      The fourth component belongs to this repository and advances when the façade changes while
      both underlying package sets stay put.

      THIS IS THE 3.x LINE of both native SDKs. Moving here from 2.x rewrote both platform
      implementations and left the public API alone - dd-sdk-android replaced OpenTracing with
      DatadogTracing/DatadogSpan, and dd-sdk-ios redistributed the DD* types out of DatadogObjc
      into one namespace per module. See docs/upgrade-to-3x.md for the full diff and
      docs/native-surface.md for what is covered.
    -->
    <DatadogNativeVersion>3.14.0</DatadogNativeVersion>
    <DatadogBindingRevision>4</DatadogBindingRevision>
    <VersionPrefix>$(DatadogNativeVersion).$(DatadogBindingRevision)</VersionPrefix>

    <!--
      The iOS and Android binding package sets this façade is compiled against. Pinned to a single
      version - which NuGet packs as a ">= x.y.z.n" MINIMUM, not an exact requirement. Default
      resolution lands on the pin, and that is the arrangement being promised: the façade calls
      into the hand-written convenience layers in both repositories (RumMonitorExtensions,
      DDRUMMonitor.Ergonomics and friends), which are not covered by any compatibility promise
      beyond "the pinned revision has them". A consumer who adds a direct reference to a NEWER
      binding floats above the pin - deliberately allowed, so an emergency binding patch is
      consumable before the façade re-pins - and the README's "running ahead of the pins" section
      states exactly what that does and does not keep working.

      The API floor is what revision .2 added: the convenience layers carry the members this
      repository used to shim for itself - the per-value attribute converters
      (DatadogAttributes.ToJava / ToNSObject), GetCurrentSessionIdAsync on both monitors, header
      injection on both tracers, and GetTraceId / GetSpanId on iOS, which is where the trace-id
      reassembly lives - so anything below .2 will not build this. The pins sit on the current
      releases above that floor; the .3 (and Mac .2) revisions are documentation, packaging and
      supply-chain releases over the same native SDKs and the same API.
    -->
    <DatadogNetiOSVersion>3.14.0.3</DatadogNetiOSVersion>
    <DatadogNetAndroidVersion>3.12.1.3</DatadogNetAndroidVersion>
    <DatadogAndroidNativeVersion>3.12.1</DatadogAndroidNativeVersion>

    <!--
      The Mac Catalyst binding package set, from the DatadogNet.Mac repository. Same native SDK as
      DatadogNetiOSVersion - dd-sdk-ios is the SDK that runs on Catalyst - but built from source
      there, since Datadog publishes no Catalyst binaries, and revisioned independently. The two
      must wrap the same dd-sdk-ios release (first three components) for the shared Platforms/iOS
      implementation to be honest about what it calls into; the fourth component is each
      repository's own.
    -->
    <DatadogNetMacVersion>3.14.0.2</DatadogNetMacVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Target frameworks: net8, net9 and net10, in two pack passes.

    Each .NET SDK's android/ios workloads ship reference packs for the current target framework and
    the previous one - the .NET 9 band builds net8 + net9, the .NET 10 band builds net9 + net10 -
    so no single SDK builds all three. BuildNugets.sh packs twice and merge-packages.py grafts the
    net10 assets into the net9 packages, exactly as both platform repositories do.

    net8 therefore comes out of the same pass as net9 - that band's android/ios workloads carry both
    reference packs - so it costs no extra pack pass. The three target-framework versions are the
    ones both platform binding package sets ship, and the façade matches them.

    ONE KNOWN LIMITATION, measured rather than assumed. A .NET *MAUI* app on net8 does not build,
    and no target-framework choice here can fix it. Pointing the sample at net8.0-android34.0 fails
    during Java callable wrapper generation:

        error XA4204: Unable to resolve interface type
        'AndroidX.Navigation.NavController/IOnDestinationChangedListener'

    MAUI 8 resolves as 8.0.100 - the original November 2023 release, since no serviced MAUI 8 exists
    in any installed workload band, and MAUI 8 reached end of support on 14 May 2025 - and its
    AndroidX generation does not line up with the one the Datadog Android bindings require. It is
    the same class of failure DatadogNet.Android's README records for MAUI 9, one version earlier
    and with a different symptom.

    So the net8 assets are for a plain .NET Android or .NET iOS app - one with no MAUI - which is
    also what DatadogNet.Android's and DatadogNet.iOS's own net8 support is for. DatadogNet.Maui
    ships net8 too, for uniformity and so that a net8 MAUI app fails with a comprehensible Android
    SDK error rather than a restore error blaming the wrong thing, but it cannot be used from one
    until MAUI's own AndroidX generation catches up.

    The device checks run against the net8 assets on both platforms, so what net8 *does* support is
    verified rather than inferred.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DatadogSdkBand Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == '' ">net9</DatadogSdkBand>

    <DatadogNetVersionMoniker Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net9' ">net8.0;net9.0</DatadogNetVersionMoniker>
    <DatadogWindowsTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net9' ">net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0;net9.0-windows10.0.19041.0</DatadogWindowsTargetFramework>
    <DatadogAndroidTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net9' ">net8.0-android34.0;net9.0-android35.0</DatadogAndroidTargetFramework>
    <DatadogiOSTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net9' ">net8.0-ios18.0;net9.0-ios18.0</DatadogiOSTargetFramework>
    <DatadogMacTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net9' ">net8.0-maccatalyst18.0;net9.0-maccatalyst18.0</DatadogMacTargetFramework>

    <DatadogNetVersionMoniker Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net10' ">net10.0</DatadogNetVersionMoniker>
    <DatadogWindowsTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net10' ">net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</DatadogWindowsTargetFramework>
    <DatadogAndroidTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net10' ">net10.0-android36.0</DatadogAndroidTargetFramework>
    <DatadogiOSTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net10' ">net10.0-ios26.0</DatadogiOSTargetFramework>
    <DatadogMacTargetFramework Condition=" '$(DatadogSdkBand)' == 'net10' ">net10.0-maccatalyst26.0</DatadogMacTargetFramework>

    <!--
      The plain net9.0/net10.0 asset is not padding. A MAUI app routinely also has a Windows or Mac
      Catalyst head, and NuGet resolves each head independently: without a platform-neutral asset,
      restoring DatadogNet on net9.0-windows10.0.19041.0 fails with NU1202 and the app has to guard
      every PackageReference by target platform. With it, the reference is unconditional and the
      Windows head links an implementation whose every member is a documented no-op. See
      Platforms/Unsupported.
    -->
    <DatadogTargetFrameworks>$(DatadogNetVersionMoniker);$(DatadogAndroidTargetFramework);$(DatadogiOSTargetFramework);$(DatadogMacTargetFramework)</DatadogTargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      12.2 on iOS because the Datadog frameworks are Swift and use the OS-provided Swift runtime,
      which is only ABI-stable from 12.2.

      API 23 on Android, and NOT the 21 the dd-sdk-android .aar manifests declare. Those .aars do
      say 21, but they depend on an AndroidX generation that does not: androidx.savedstate 1.4.0
      declares minSdkVersion 23, and the Android manifest merger takes the maximum across the whole
      graph rather than trusting the direct dependency. An app declaring 21 therefore fails to build
      outright, before anything runs:

          uses-sdk:minSdkVersion 21 cannot be smaller than version 23 declared in library
          ... androidx.savedstate.savedstate-android.aar

      DatadogNet.Android's README records the floor as 21 for this reason, and treats it as a
      practical argument for the 2.x line over 3.0's 23. That argument does not survive contact with
      the transitive graph: 23 is the real floor either way.

      The suggested `tools:overrideLibrary="androidx.savedstate"` escape is deliberately not taken.
      It forces the library in and, as the error itself says, may fail at runtime instead - which is
      a worse trade for the two API levels it buys.
    -->
    <DatadogSupportedIosVersion>12.2</DatadogSupportedIosVersion>
    <DatadogSupportedAndroidVersion>23</DatadogSupportedAndroidVersion>
    <!--
      15.0 (macOS 12) on Mac Catalyst, matching the DatadogNet.Mac binding packages: it is both
      the floor the .NET 9 maccatalyst workload enforces for apps and the macOS line Datadog's
      own Catalyst support statement starts at.
    -->
    <DatadogSupportedMacCatalystVersion>15.0</DatadogSupportedMacCatalystVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>false</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <CheckEolTargetFramework>false</CheckEolTargetFramework>
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>s.bokatuk</Authors>
    <Company>s.bokatuk</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright © s.bokatuk</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/sbokatuk/DatadogNet</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/sbokatuk/DatadogNet</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <!--
      The same expression the two binding repositories declare, and for the same reason: the code
      here is MIT, and every package it depends on ships native artifacts Datadog publishes under
      Apache-2.0. NuGet resolves a licence per package, not per graph, so a package whose
      dependencies carry Apache-2.0 payloads has to say so itself.
    -->
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT AND Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>false</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>

    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition=" '$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true' ">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Surface the curated release notes on nuget.org. docs/release-notes/<version>.md already feeds
    the GitHub release; packing it here is what makes it reach the .nupkg, so nuget.org stops
    showing empty release notes. A version with no curated file - a PR prerelease of unreleased
    work, typically - falls back to the releases page rather than shipping nothing.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DatadogReleaseNotesFile>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)docs/release-notes/$(VersionPrefix).md</DatadogReleaseNotesFile>
    <PackageReleaseNotes Condition=" Exists('$(DatadogReleaseNotesFile)') ">$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(DatadogReleaseNotesFile)'))</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageReleaseNotes Condition=" '$(PackageReleaseNotes)' == '' ">https://github.com/sbokatuk/DatadogNet/releases/tag/v$(VersionPrefix)</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
